          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \in \mathbb{R}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/length"} length
            of one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hemisphere"} semi-circle
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+circle"} unit circle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \pi
            is the circumference of the full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+circle"} unit circle
          %p
            This number plays a central role in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trigonometry"} trigonometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analysis"} complex analysis
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler%27s+formula"} Euler's formula
            – it also appears in many other situations.
          %h2#definition_via_real_analysis Definition via real analysis
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \in \mathbb{R}
            (occasionally called
            %em Ludolph’s number
            —make that
            %em very
            occasionally, nowadays— or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em Archimedes’ constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+real+number"} positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/root"} root
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sine+function"} sine function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi sin
                      %mi ℝ
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin_\mathbb{R} : \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+value+problem"} initial value problem
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi sin
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mo ″
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi sin
                    %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin_\mathbb{R}'' = -sin_\mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi sin
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin_\mathbb{R}(0) = 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi sin
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin_\mathbb{R}'(0) = 1 .
          %p
            Alternatively,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            may be defined as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2} \tau
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/period"} period
            of the same function
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sin"} sin
          %p
            An alternative description of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin
            is as the unique function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}
            continuous at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and otherwise satisfying
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≤
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi sin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 - x^2 \leq \frac{sin(x)}{x} \leq 1
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sin
                  %mn 3
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mn 3
                  %mi sin
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mn 4
                  %msup
                    %mi sin
                    %mn 3
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin 3 x = 3 sin x - 4 sin^3 x
          %p
            although this makes practical calculations rather difficult. (If you really care to know why this characterization works, you can look
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/toddtrimble/published/Characterization+of+sine"} here
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/5773/a-short-note-for-todd-trimble/#Item_4"} this nForum discussion
          %h3#tangential_remarks Tangential Remarks
          %p
            Either of the definitions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin
            above mentions a thing, and claims some properties for it, in order to indirectly describe some other thing (in this case,
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            to make sense — particularly, that the roots of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin
            are isolated, and that there is a positive root. These might make a decent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
            in a first course in classical analysis.
          %h2#definition_via_complex_analysis Definition via complex analysis
          %p
            A related but perhaps more conceptual description is via complex analysis. Recall that the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+function"} exponential function
            is defined by the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi z
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mn 3
                    %mn 6
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(z) = \sum_{n \geq 0} \frac{z^n}{n!} = 1 + z + \frac{z^2}{2} + \frac{z^3}{6} + \ldots
          %p
            for all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            (as an absolutely
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} convergent series
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo +
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(z + w) = \exp(z) \cdot \exp(w)
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi z
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\widebar{z}) = \widebar{\exp(z)}
                \.
          %p
            It follows that for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi i
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in i \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo +
                    %mover
                      %mi z
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z + \widebar{z} = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\exp(i t)|} = 1
          %p
            Hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \mapsto \exp(i t)
            defines a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            from the additive group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to the multiplicative group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            of unit complex numbers. It may be checked that this is a local diffeomorphism: a local inverse to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \mapsto \exp(z) - 1
            in a neighborhood of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi z
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mn 2
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mn 3
                    %mn 3
                  %mo −
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \mapsto \log (1 + z) = z - \frac{z^2}{2} + \frac{z^3}{3} - \ldots
          %p
            Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: \mathbb{R} \to S^1
            maps a small neighborhood
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            onto a small neighborhood
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 = \bigcup_{n \geq 1} V^n
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^n
            consists of
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi m
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_{n=1}^m V^n
            suffices since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: \mathbb{R} \to S^1
            is surjective.
          %p
            In particular, there exists some element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbb{R}
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(t) = -1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            to be the least positive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(t) = -1
            (cf. Euler’s formula
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{\pi i} + 1 = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ker(\phi)
            is nontrivial (since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+group"} torsion elements
            whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            does not) and must be a discrete subgroup, generated by a unique positive
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo =
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi = \tau/2
          %p
            These considerations suggest why mathematicians sometimes consider
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau = 2\pi
            to be “more fundamental” than
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\pi i
            is the truly fundamental quantity, being (up to sign) the unique generator of the kernel of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp: \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}^\times
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi z
                    %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{{|z|} = 1} \frac{d z}{z} = 2\pi i
          %p that on some level pervades complex analysis.
          %h2#archimedean_definition “Archimedean” definition
          %p
            A sequence that approximates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            that was available to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedes"} Archimedes
            (using his “method of exhaustion”) involves calculating perimeters of inscribed
            = succeed "-gons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^n
          %p
            Define a sequence by the recursion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_{n+1} = \sqrt{2 + x_n}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 4
                    %mo =
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mo +
                            %msqrt
                              %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_4 = \sqrt{2 + \sqrt{2 + \sqrt{2}}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            in the limit; put
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_n = 2^n \sqrt{2 - x_n}.
          %p
            The statement is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_n
            is half the perimeter of the inscribed
            = succeed "-gon" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{n+1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_n
            is an increasing convergent sequence which tends to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            in the limit. Incidentally, simple high-school algebra shows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_n = 2 \prod_{k=2}^n \frac{2}{x_k}
          %p and thus we may deduce Vieta’s formula (see below).
          %p
            Archimedes used a similar idea, in effect viewing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\pi
            as squeezed between the perimeters of inscribed and circumscribed polygons with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3 \cdot 2^n
            sides each (starting with a hexagon). By considering
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 5
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 10
                      %mn 71
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi π
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 3
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3 + \frac{10}{71} \lt \pi \lt 3 + \frac1{7}
          %h2#other_formulas Other formulas
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Leibniz formula:
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mfrac
                          %mi π
                          %mn 4
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 1
                          %mn 3
                        %mo +
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 1
                          %mn 5
                        %mo −
                        %mi …
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\pi}{4} = 1 - \frac1{3} + \frac1{5} - \ldots
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arctangent"} arctangent
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi arctan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                        %mn 3
                      %mo +
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 5
                        %mn 5
                      %mo −
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \arctan(x) = x - \frac{x^3}{3} + \frac{x^5}{5} - \ldots,
              %p
                this series for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi/4
                obviously converges slowly, but more sophisticated expressions in terms of the arctangent have better convergence rates, such as the following first observed by Machin:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mi π
                        %mn 4
                      %mo =
                      %mn 4
                      %mi arctan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 5
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi arctan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 239
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\pi}{4} = 4\arctan(\frac1{5}) - \arctan(\frac1{239})
            %li
              %p
                Integral formulas (based ultimately on the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+integral"} Riemann integral
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henstock+integral"} Henstock integral
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+integral"} Lebesgue integral
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    Also based on the
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arctangent"} arctangent
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi π
                          %mo =
                          %mn 4
                          %msubsup
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                            %mn 1
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                              %mi x
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi x
                                %mn 2
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo .
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi = 4 \int_{x=0}^1 \frac{\mathrm{d}x}{x^2 + 1} .
                %li
                  %p
                    Or the
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %span.newWikiWord
                        arcsine
                        %a{:href => "/nlab/new/arcsine"} ?
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi π
                          %mo =
                          %mn 2
                          %msubsup
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                            %mn 1
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                              %mi x
                            %msqrt
                              %mrow
                                %mn 1
                                %mo −
                                %msup
                                  %mi x
                                  %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi = 2 \int_{x=0}^1 \frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\sqrt{1 - x^2}}
                  %p
                    (but this is an
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      improper integral
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/improper+integral"} ?
                    if one is using the
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+integral"} Riemann integral
              %p Of course, you can come up with many modifications of these (including proper Riemann integrals based on the arcsine).
            %li
              %p Product formulas:
              %ul
                %li Vieta’s formula:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 2
                    %msqrt
                      %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 2
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msqrt
                          %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 2
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mo +
                            %msqrt
                              %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 2
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mo +
                            %msqrt
                              %mrow
                                %mn 2
                                %mo +
                                %msqrt
                                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi= 2\times\frac{2}{\sqrt{2}}\times
                  \\frac{2}{\sqrt{2+\sqrt{2}}}\times
                  \\frac{2}{\sqrt{2+\sqrt{2+\sqrt{2}}}}\times\frac{2}{\sqrt{2+\sqrt{2+\sqrt{2+\sqrt{2}}}}}\times\cdots
          %p
            This was perhaps the first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+product"} infinite product
            in the history of mathematics.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Euler’s
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+formula+for+the+sine+function"} product formula
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi sin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi π
                            %mn 2
                          %msup
                            %mi n
                            %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sin(x) = x \prod_{n \geq 1} (1 - \frac{x^2}{\pi^2 n^2})
              %p
                One point is that putting
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mfrac
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = \frac{\pi}{2}
            %li
              %p Wallis’s formula:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo →
                          %mn ∞
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 2
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 2
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 4
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 4
                        %mn 5
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 6
                        %mn 5
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 6
                        %mn 7
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\pi}{2} = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{2}{1} \cdot \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{4}{3} \cdot \frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{6}{5} \cdot \frac{6}{7} \cdot \ldots \cdot \frac{2 n}{2 n - 1} \cdot \frac{2 n}{2 n + 1}
              %p
                Alternatively, this may be derived from
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stirling%27s+approximation"} Stirling's approximation
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo !
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n!
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mi n
                          %mi n
                        %mo )
                      %mo ∼
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mn 2
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi n
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \binom{2 n}{n} \sim \frac{2^{2 n}}{\sqrt{\pi n}}.
            %li
              %p
                Euler series: let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ζ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta(s)
                be the Riemann
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function"} zeta function
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cot
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mn 2
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 1
                      %mfrac
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi π
                            %mn 2
                          %msup
                            %mi n
                            %mn 2
                          %mo −
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cot(x) = \frac1{x} - 2\sum_{n \geq 1} \frac{x}{\pi^2 n^2 - x^2}.
              %p Expanding each of the summands in a geometric series and rearranging terms, one may derive
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi cot
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mn 2
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 1
                      %mi ζ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi m
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi π
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi m
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \cot(x) = 1 - 2\sum_{m \geq 1} \zeta(2 m)\frac{x^{2m}}{\pi^{2 m}}.
              %p On the other hand, one has
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi cot
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi i
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi i
                          %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi e
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi i
                              %mi x
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi i
                          %mi x
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi n
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\cot(x) = i x + \frac{2 i x}{e^{2 i x}-1} = 1 + \sum_{n \geq 2} B_n \frac{(2 i x)^n}{n!}
              %p
                where the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_n
                are the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bernoulli+numbers"} Bernoulli numbers
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi π
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi m
                      %mi ζ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi m
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mn 2
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi m
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo !
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\frac{2}{\pi^{2 m}} \zeta(2 m) = (-1)^m \frac{2^{2 m}}{(2 m)!}B_{2 m}
              %p or, after rearranging,
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi π
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo !
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mn 2
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi k
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi k
                      %mi ζ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^{2 k} = (-1)^{k-1} \frac{(2 k)!}{2^{2 k - 1}B_{2 k}}\zeta(2 k)
              %p
                which has as special cases the formulas
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mn 6
                        %mi ζ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^2 = 6\zeta(2)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 4
                        %mo =
                        %mn 90
                        %mi ζ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 4
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^4 = 90\zeta(4)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 6
                        %mo =
                        %mn 945
                        %mi ζ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 6
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^6 = 945\zeta(6)
            %li
              %p
                Continued fractions: the regular
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continued+fraction"} continued fraction
                expansion of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is highly irregular, but several generalized continued fraction expansions listed in
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pi#Continued_fractions"} Wikipedia
                include
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 4
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                            %mo +
                            %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                              %mfrac
                                %mrow
                                  %msup
                                    %mn 1
                                    %mn 2
                                %mrow
                                  %mn 2
                                  %mo +
                                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                    %mfrac
                                      %mrow
                                        %msup
                                          %mn 3
                                          %mn 2
                                      %mrow
                                        %mn 2
                                        %mo +
                                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                          %mfrac
                                            %mrow
                                              %msup
                                                %mn 5
                                                %mn 2
                                            %mrow
                                              %mn 2
                                              %mo +
                                              %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                                %mfrac
                                                  %mrow
                                                    %msup
                                                      %mn 7
                                                      %mn 2
                                                  %mrow
                                                    %mn 2
                                                    %mo +
                                                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                                      %mfrac
                                                        %mrow
                                                          %msup
                                                            %mn 9
                                                            %mn 2
                                                        %mrow
                                                          %mn 2
                                                          %mo +
                                                          %mi ⋱
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\pi=\textstyle \frac{4}{1+\textstyle \frac{1^2}{2+\textstyle \frac{3^2}{2+\textstyle \frac{5^2}{2+\textstyle \frac{7^2}{2+\textstyle \frac{9^2}{2+\ddots}}}}}}
          %h2#irrationality_and_transcendence Irrationality and transcendence
          %p
            Famously,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irrational+number"} irrational number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continued+fraction"} continued fraction
            expression for the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent"} tangent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi tan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi z
                                %mn 2
                            %mrow
                              %mn 3
                              %mo −
                              %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                %mfrac
                                  %mrow
                                    %msup
                                      %mi z
                                      %mn 2
                                  %mrow
                                    %mn 5
                                    %mo −
                                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                      %mfrac
                                        %mrow
                                          %msup
                                            %mi z
                                            %mn 2
                                        %mrow
                                          %mn 7
                                          %mo −
                                          %mi …
                    %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tan(z) = \textstyle \frac{z}{1 - \textstyle \frac{z^2}{3 - \textstyle \frac{z^2}{5 - \textstyle \frac{z^2}{7 - \ldots}}}}.
          %p
            The basic inference was that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi tan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tan(z)
            must therefore be irrational for rational values of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi tan
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tan(\pi/4) = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi/4
            is irrational. This was not a completely rigorous proof since Lambert had failed to prove the irrationality of certain infinite continued fractions, but Legendre provided the missing lemma in 1806. Actually
            %a{:href => "#Leg"} Legendre
            proves more: applying his lemma to the continued fraction for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msqrt
                    %mi q
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi tan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msqrt
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{q}\; \tan(\sqrt{q})
            (derived from the above), he shows this expression is irrational for rational
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^2
            can be rational. He goes on to speculate that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            is in fact transcendental.
          %p
            The transcendence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            was finally established by Lindemann in 1882, by adapting methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Hermite"} Charles Hermite
            who had established the transcendence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/e"} e
            in 1873. This definitively proved the impossibility of solving the problem of “squaring the circle” by means of ruler-and-compass constructions. A far more general theorem was then established by Weierstrass:
          #LindWeier.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n
              are algebraic numbers linearly independent over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\alpha_1), \ldots, \exp(\alpha_n)
              are
              %em algebraically
              independent over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              (which is to say that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}(\exp(\alpha_1), \ldots, \exp(\alpha_n))
              has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transcendence+degree"} transcendence degree
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              over
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
          %p
            A proof is given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof_that_%CF%80_is_transcendental#Proof"} Wikipedia
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            and therefore
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\pi
            were algebraic, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i\pi) = -1
            would be transcendental, which is absurd.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circumference+of+a+circle"} circumference of a circle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+constant"} circle constant
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/e"} e
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/golden+ratio"} golden ratio
          %h2#references References
          %p
            A whole chapter on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            written for the mathematically literate is given in
          %ul
            %li
              Ebbinghaus et al.,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Numbers
          %p
            The material on the Euler series for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^{2 n}
            in terms of the zeta function was extracted from
          %ul
            %li
              Kenneth Ireland and Michael Rosen,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Classical Introduction to Modern Number Theory
          %p
            The proof of irrationality of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            given by Legendre is said to be given in the following treatise:
          %ul
            %li#Leg
              A.-M. Legendre,
              %em Eléments de géométrie
              (11th edition), Note IV. F. Didot, Paris (1817). Translation by Charles Davies: (
              %a{:href => "http://quod.lib.umich.edu/cgi/t/text/text-idx?c=moa;idno=ABN7066.0001.001"}> web
              )
          %p#BBPFormulaReferences
            On efficient representations in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+real+computer+arithmetic"} exact real computer arithmetic
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pi"} pi
            and related
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irrational+numbers"} irrational numbers
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bailey-Borwein-Plouffe+algorithm"}> Bailey-Borwein-Plouffe algorithm
            )
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Bailey"}> David Bailey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Borwein"} Peter Borwein
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Plouffe"} Simon Plouffe
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Rapid Computation of Various Polylogarithmic Constants
              %strong 66
              (1997) 903-913 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/journals/mcom/1997-66-218/S0025-5718-97-00856-9"}> doi:1997-66-218/S0025-5718-97-00856-9
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/digits.pdf"} pdf
          %p further discussed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jerzy+Karczmarczuk"}> Jerzy Karczmarczuk
                ,
                %em Infinite precision real fractions, and lazy carry propagation
                or:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    The Most Unreliable Technique in the World to Compute
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi π
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://karczmarczuk.users.greyc.fr/arpap/lazypi.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Karczmarczuk-Pi.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Plouffe"}> Simon Plouffe
                ,
                %em
                  On the computation of the
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi n
                          %mi th
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
                  decimal digit of various transcendental numbers
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.0303"}> arXiv:0912.0303
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                MathWorld,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/BBP-TypeFormula.html"} BBP-Type formula
